From: Eduardo Garcia-Madico Portabella <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: ipa: bound the wait for a transaction to quiesce

gsi_channel_trans_quiesce() waits for the last underway transaction with
wait_for_completion(), with no timeout. If the transaction is never going to
complete -- which is what happens when the modem goes down underneath us --
that wait never returns, and it is held on the runtime PM suspend path.

The result is a deadlock between the suspend and the device's own interrupt
thread. Both stacks, captured on a Xiaomi POCO X3 NFC (sm7150) running 7.1:

  kworker/u32:5           irq/172-ipa
  (Workqueue: pm pm_runtime_work)
   ipa_runtime_suspend     ipa_isr_thread
    ipa_endpoint_suspend    __pm_runtime_resume
     ipa_modem_suspend       rpm_resume
      gsi_channel_suspend     schedule        <- blocked
       __gsi_channel_stop
        gsi_channel_trans_quiesce
         wait_for_completion  <- never returns

The suspend waits for a completion that can only be delivered by processing
the channel; the interrupt thread that would do that is blocked waiting for
the in-flight runtime PM transition to finish. Neither side yields.

With hung_task_panic enabled this shows up as:

  INFO: task kworker/u32:5:100 blocked for more than 122 seconds.
  INFO: task irq/172-ipa:874 blocked for more than 123 seconds.
  Kernel panic - not syncing: hung_task: blocked tasks

and, because the trigger persists while the modem stays down, as a reboot
loop: the machine came back, tried to suspend IPA again, and panicked again
about two minutes later, over and over, until the network returned.

Give the wait a bound. A transaction that is making progress completes in
milliseconds, so two seconds is generous; past that the far end is not going
to answer. On timeout we log and carry on to gsi_channel_stop_retry(), which
already knows how to report failure to stop a channel, so an eternal hang
becomes a recoverable error.

The reference taken by gsi_channel_trans_last() is dropped either way.
Transactions are refcounted, so releasing ours is safe even if hardware
completes the transaction afterwards.
---
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/gsi.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/gsi.c
@@ -807,6 +807,13 @@
 }
 
 /* Wait for transaction activity on a channel to complete */
+/* How long to wait for an underway transaction to complete before giving up.
+ * A transaction that is making progress completes in milliseconds; anything
+ * beyond this means the far end is not going to answer -- typically because
+ * the modem went down underneath us.
+ */
+#define TRANS_QUIESCE_TIMEOUT_MS	2000
+
 static void gsi_channel_trans_quiesce(struct gsi_channel *channel)
 {
 	struct gsi_trans *trans;
@@ -814,7 +821,16 @@
 	/* Get the last transaction, and wait for it to complete */
 	trans = gsi_channel_trans_last(channel);
 	if (trans) {
-		wait_for_completion(&trans->completion);
+		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&trans->completion,
+						 msecs_to_jiffies(TRANS_QUIESCE_TIMEOUT_MS)))
+			dev_err(channel->gsi->dev,
+				"channel %u: timed out waiting for transaction to complete\n",
+				gsi_channel_id(channel));
+
+		/* Drop our reference either way; the transaction is
+		 * refcounted, so releasing it here is safe even if hardware
+		 * completes it later.
+		 */
 		gsi_trans_free(trans);
 	}
 }
